    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "Across the spectrum German outlets converge on the assessment that the war in Ukraine has made a rapid overhaul of the Bundeswehr unavoidable, stressing the urgency of higher defence budgets to reach NATO’s 2 percent goal and to fund modern air‑defence, cyber and autonomous weapon systems. They also uniformly highlight the political pressure to revive a form of general service, whether through a shortened conscription or a civilian reserve, as a means to address personnel shortages and to reinforce the societal legitimacy of the armed forces.",
      "controversial_points": "The core conflict pits left‑liberal anti‑militarism against right‑conservative calls for stronger defence and NATO loyalty. A second tension divides those demanding increased spending to meet the 2 % NATO target from those protecting social welfare budgets. Conscription revival creates a clash between security advocates seeking citizen soldiers and civil‑rights groups fearing militarisation of society. Arms‑export debates pit economic interests and strategic alliances against pacifist criticism of Germany’s role in global conflicts."
    }
